on the 11th of November 1918 what had  been the biggest conflict so far in  human history finally came to an end  Germany capitulated and signed the  Treaty of Versailles the war changed the  world especially in Eastern Europe where  three empires crumbled new States  emerged new ideologies too and in many  places there was more bloody conflict  this is the story of the First World War  told from the Eastern European  perspective four great powers failed to  survive the war they even disappeared  from the map austro-hungary and the  Russian German and ottoman empires all  fell apart new states emerged in Eastern  Europe nearly all began feuding with  their neighbors over ideology territory  maritime access and natural resources  one ideology came to the fore communism  the first world war was a disaster for  Russia that Tsar had plenty of troops  but lacked equipment from the start the  Russians were not fully committed to the  war morale collapsed fully in 1917 that  Tsar was overthrown the communist party  took power and sealed a peace treaty  with Germany but it didn't bring peace  Russia plunged into a civil war lasting  years  populations in the South wanted to set  up their own nation-states Bosnian Serbs  Croats and Slovenes but then a secret  agreement between Italy and the  victorious powers came to light in  return for their assistance in the war  the Italians were promised strategic  assets in Croatia Italy claimed Croatian  ports and harbours right where Croatia  planned to set up its own state in 1919  the southern Europeans had to change  course to fend off the Italians they  established a joint umbrella state the  kingdom of Yugoslavia but the  populations of this new entity did not  get on well together unrest seethed for  decades breaking out into a bloody civil  war 75 years later now the people of  southern Europe have their own national  identities the founding of Ukraine was  equally convoluted as soon as the war  ended Ukraine fell into dispute with  Poland one argument was over the city of  l''ve if it was Eastern Europe in a  microcosm poles lived in the center of  live if Ukrainians in the area around  the city so where was the border to be  drawn l'viv became polish today it's  once again Ukrainian just as with  Ukraine and Poland border disputes in  Eastern Europe often hinged on  nationality villages a few kilometers  apart often spoke different languages  people went to separate schools and sold  themselves as part of another country  Eastern Europe is still preoccupied by  where borders should be drawn feelings  of national identity rarely accord with  the lines on a map it was a similar  story in Bulgaria which found itself on  the losing side in the first world war  the country had to give away territory  make payments to the victors and take in  large numbers of refugees from the  border areas so - in Hungary which lost  well over half of its sovereign  territory Europe became aware of its  ethnic minorities once the war ended the  need to protect them was evident and  remains so today  six months after the end of the war  Romania started another attacking  Hungary and regaining parts of the  country which still supports a Hungarian  minority as it happened Romania reverted  communism in Hungary to communist leader  béla kun fled following the fall of  Budapest when the Romanians withdrew  Hungarian army seized power after the  first world war Poland regained its  national territory following more than  200 years under foreign occupation three  and a half million Ukrainians also lived  there a million Russians and a million  Germans the post-war order was committed  to protecting these minorities it was a  difficult and unpopular undertaking for  the Polish government and the cause of  much strife poles had no desire to build  schools and universities for Ukrainians  and Germans so 100 years on is the  impact of the First World War still  being felt in today's Eastern Europe for  certainly border disputes continue to  torment the region between Serbia and  Kosovo for example and the emergence of  new states has meant new minorities and  interned the scourge of discrimination  the truce of 1918 might have ended what  was sometimes called the Great War but  it didn't bring lasting peace to the  people of Central and Eastern Europe
 

